The problem of changing the style features of an image while preserving the content features, known as style-transfer or image-to-image translation, has been one of the most extensively studied questions in image synthesis research. There are many variants of this problem, depending on the data available during training and testing.

Standard style-transfer \cite{gatys2015neural, gatys2016image, huang2017arbitrary, karras2019style} involves training an image generation network (autoencoder or GAN) on a general unlabelled image dataset, and then exploiting the hierarchical structure of the network to separate high-level content features from low-level texture features. The Adaptive Instance Normalization framework \cite{huang2017arbitrary} achieves this by modifying the statistics of the low-level features of the input content image to match the statistics of the input style image.

Supervised image-to-image translation methods \cite{isola2017image} train a conditional image generation network on a dataset with paired images from two different domains. Unsupervised methods \cite{zhu2017unpaired, liu2017unsupervised}, can be trained to map between two domains in the same way, but without having seen paired examples. Multi-domain unsupervised methods \cite{Huang_2018_ECCV, choi2018stargan, liu2019few, Choi_2020_CVPR} can accommodate a variable number of domains. However, they rely on the same separation of low-level and high-level features through AdaIN layers \cite{huang2017arbitrary} in order to facilitate the translation.

The problem with this reliance on the hierarchical structure of deep nets to achieve translation is twofold: Firstly, it is a heuristic which is not always appropriate to the problem of interest, since very often the style features are at the same level as the content features, such as in the case of font transfer \ref{fig:font}. Secondly, and more subtly, low-level texture statistics and high-level content features are conditionally independent given an image, which means that they are actually marginally dependent (see Figure \ref{fig:bayes_net}). The implication of this fact is that, when switching the texture statistics for the purpose of style transfer, the resulting content-texture feature combination might be outside the manifold of the two factors, and so produce an image which appears incoherent. This is what happens in the case of highly structured datasets where the style and content are entangled strongly, such as the problem of transferring the style of a piece of furniture on the structure of another piece of furniture \ref{fig:chair}. The unsuitability of AdaIN for these kinds of problems can be seen in Figures \ref{fig:arch}, \ref{fig:font}.

Recent work has partially overcome this limitation by learning separate style and content representations \cite{kotovenko2019content, Kotovenko_2019_ICCV}. However, the method still relies on heuristic losses to encourage this separation, such as the triplet loss \cite{hermans2017defense} and the content reconstruction loss \cite{liu2019few}. While these losses are effective, they suffer from great learning instability, and dependent on the careful choice of hyperparameters, whose values are strongly dataset dependent. Instead, we want a more principled learning algorithm for disentanglement that makes as few assumptions as possible about the data.

\subsection{Proposed approach}

I believe in designing general learning algorithms which have as little inbuilt knowledge as possible, but which can be trained on a specific problem to learn the biases and structure of the data. Consequently, I think latent disentanglement with a probabilistic foundation is particularly suited to a problem such as combining style and content in furniture design. Such a method would be trained on an IKEA catalog with the images grouped either by style or by function. Hierarchical graph methods on point clouds \cite{mo2019structurenet, mo2020structedit} can already perform this task well for 3D representations of furniture. We would like a method which could perform as well for images, without hard-coding assumptions about the 3D nature of the depicted object.

Our Domain-Content model is, in theory, directly applicable to this problem. However, it has, so far, only been applied to datasets with, justifiably, small and simple images, such as Silhouettes (Figure \ref{fig:silhouettes}) or Fonts (Figure \ref{fig:fonts}). I expect it to, initially, produce images of poor quality when trained on the IKEA catalog because of the size of the images and the complexity of the task. This is why many image synthesis algorithms use skip-connections, or other stabilising techniques. I intend to research some general approaches that allow the network to produce outputs with detail at different scales without exploiting the pixel structure of the images too much. One option would be to extend the Domain-Content model to learn autoregressive latent representations through a recurrent network that can iteratively refine the  outputs, such as in Generative Query Networks \cite{eslami2018neural}. Another approach would be to emulate StructNet \cite{mo2019structurenet} and design learned a hierarchical graph-based semantic representation over pixel that would allow the progressive addition of texture information with each layer in a way that reflects the semantic structure of the data and not the absolute scale within the image.

This would be a short to medium-term project, depending how well the Domain-Content model would perform on the furniture problem. If the model, with the proposed improvements, works well, I would aim to submit this work as a paper for CVPR 2021 in mid-Novemeber 2020. The main steps of this project are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item To gather datasets appropriate for this task, such as an IKEA furniture dataset, and structure it into packs corresponding to the Domain-Content frameworks.
\item To design a set of variations of the Domain-Content model, as discussed above, and compare them with the a state-of-the-art image-to-image translation method, such as StarGAN \cite{Choi_2020_CVPR}.
\end{enumerate}

