Another new and highly interesting problem is the separate representation and manipulation of objects in a scene. The formulation is the following: at test time, given an image of an object and an image of a scene, insert that object into the scene at a location specified by an input segmentation map. The insertion must be done in a way that preserves the coherence of the scene (this may involve modifying either the object, the scene, or both). Alternatively, remove an object from the scene located at a position highlighted on the input segmentation map. The procedure is exemplified in Figure \ref{fig:remove_insert}.

Matting and compositing are a related computational photography problem that involve cropping a source object from a source scene and applying it to a target scene. This area of research is well established, with some new methods using deep learning to achieve excellent results with little information \cite{BMSengupta20}. The problem we are interested in, however, also involves modifying the both the source object and the target scene to account for the object insertion. 

Image inpainting is another related problem, requiring the hallucination of pixels highlighted by a segmentation map. Current deep learning methods learn the manifold of natural images and then bring the input image, close to the manifold, achieving realistic results. However, inpainting is not a reliable method for object removal (Figure \ref{fig:inpainting}), for complex scenes, since the network can only model an approximation of the relationship between the elements of the scene, as learned through very general image statistics.

Deep image-to-image translation methods approach this problem by exploiting the distinction between low-level texture and high-level structure, as discussed in the previous section. For example, Spatially Adaptive Normalization \cite{Park_2019_CVPR} (Figure \ref{fig:spade}) synthesizes different textures for different classes of objects within the image. Semantic Generation Pyramid \cite{Shocher_2020_CVPR} (Figure \ref{fig:pyramid}) is an approximate way to perform this task by simply inserting or removing an object in the scene naively, and then generating a new image with the high-level structure of the naive composition and the low-level texture of the original image. These methods work well for image types defined by general texture, such as landscapes, but fail to account for the relationship between objects in terms of lighting, occlusions, reflections, and spatial organization. The latter method also requires a precise segmentation map, which in the real-world is often impractical.

There is already substantial probabilistic ML research approaching this problem through the disentanglement of individual object representations based on the VAE paradigm. Models like IODINE \cite{pmlr-v97-greff19a}, MONet \cite{burgess2019monet} and GENESIS \cite{engelcke2019genesis} produce separate latent representations corresponding to each object in the scene, and achieve excellent results in modelling the interaction between objects when generating an image (Figure \ref{fig:iodine}). They serve as a solid foundation for the method I would like to develop.

What these methods lack is a latent representation of the arrangement of objects in the scene. This is crucial for image types where the constituent objects are spatially interlocked, such that the insertion or removal of an object changes the location of the others. This is the case, for example, with images depicting text, where each character is an object, as seen in Figure \ref{fig:remove_insert} a), b). The latent representation of scene layout would also enable the coherent repositioning of objects (Figure \ref{fig:remove_insert} d)), another useful image editing tool.

\subsection{Proposed approach}

The disentanglement methods mentioned previously do not represent the object locations in such a relative way, but in an absolute way, such as through a segmentation map. A separate latent representation can be learned by using the Domain-Content paradigm, where the Domain is the collection of objects in the scene and the Content is their arrangement. Therefore, an appropriate model for this task would combine Domain-Content with object-centric representation by breaking up the Domain code into separate object codes which could be deleted, added, or modified (Figure \ref{fig:remove_insert}).

This extension is non-trivial, as it would require integrating the two probabilistic models and learning frameworks, and designing a realistic training regimen for real-world tasks that provides enough information for this complex disentanglement. This is a medium to long-term project, and I would be aiming for a paper at ECCV 2021 or NeurIPS 2021. The main steps of this project would be:

\begin{enumerate}
\item To gather datasets of multi-object images where the disentanglement method would be most useful. That is, image data with strongly interacting objects where high precision in removal/insertion/modification is required. Such application data include architectural plans, typography and branding, diagrams, or natural indoor photography.
\item To establish the problem formulation from the perspective of training and testing. \item To synthesize the object-centric model and the Domain-Content model into a unified probabilistic model with an associated neural architecture.
\item To compare our model's performance with the state-of-the-art in both multi-object disentanglement, image-to-image translation, and the benchmarks endemic to the specific tasks approached. For example, re-arranging objects in the scene by modifying the Content code can be performed in such a way as to produce image resizing. Therefore, we should compare with the state-of-the-art seam carving techniques \cite{avidan2007seam, arar2019image}.
\end{enumerate}


